{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2334 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2171aba0ac50362b038308f33ecc31c742e7386b501c8c219d46163f5147b439" :edb/db-before-t 2333 :edb/db-after-t 2334 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535646 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:23:41.718Z" 13194139535646 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359112 13194139535646 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359115 13194139535646 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359098 13194139535646 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359105 13194139535646 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359119 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359099 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1089 "q-138910" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1100 "A body of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}-16t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1113 #uuid "1e63c32d-f7f6-5fb4-a61e-8ffdf954dfa5" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359098 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-16t+5) \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-16 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =16 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359100 13194139535646 true] [17592186359099