$v(t)=(t-4){e}^{-t}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." 13194139535646 true] [17592186359105 1113 #uuid "221d56ed-83a4-543c-a1f6-7af8b38ce036" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359105 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}+(t-4)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(5-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-t){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 5-t & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359107 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359108 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359109 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359110 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359111 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1041 "selection" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359110 13194139535646 true] [17592186359106 1110 #uuid "27cbe526-1d04-5086-ae08-08e982b90f4a" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359107 1034 "t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359107 1111 #uuid "10b4b33c-fa3a-5e34-a592-6999b9feccc7" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359108 1034 "t=4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359108 1111 #uuid "fdcc3ead-5216-5a37-8f8f-f4c2ba997801" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359109 1034 "t=8\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359109 1111 #uuid "df798ab0-d00a-55db-84d6-4b2221a0f07a" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359110 1034 "t=5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359110 1111 #uuid "3afa6e5d-8196-5565-b620-7fe7e3fe9f7b" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359111 1034 "t=10\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359111 1111 #uuid "f57f7845-d030-54c0-828e-ba3f108f18af" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359113 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1089 "q-345338" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1100 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1113 #uuid "7cd21afb-f40b-53c4-ad7d-67ffe3f6fab0" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359112 1145 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359114 13194139535646 true] [17592186359113 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535646 true] [17592186359113 1041 "field-1" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359114 13194139535646 true] [17592186359113 1110 #uuid "fc8570f7-ec30-5238-be01-255ea6bd9546" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359114 1034 "2(\\sec(t))^2" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359114 1111 #uuid "91b443ba-c052-53be-a205-e4557529460e" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359116 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1089 "q-345340" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1100 "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1113 #uuid "df1e6f0b-3cb8-5b84-956f-6e1aa296a8a0" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359115 1145 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359117 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359118 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1041 "field-1" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359118 13194139535646 true] [17592186359116 1110 #uuid "e5ae1eba-b1a1-511e-ba2d-0316cadded79" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359117 1034 "\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359117 1111 #uuid "fefe3b53-f05d-512a-a0fe-9b2334b8e141" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359118 1034 "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359118 1111 #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359120 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1089 "q-345390" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1100 "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1113 #uuid "36d3c8af-adbd-575f-a078-a90af2f53575" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359119 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-12t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(12{t}^{2}-12t+12) \\\\  & =24t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24t-12 & =0 \\\\ 24t & =12 \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359121 13194139535646 true] [17592186359120 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535646 true] [17592186359120 1041 "field-1" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359120 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359121 13194139535646 true] [17592186359120 1110 #uuid "28cd5d84-cbbc-5c67-bdf6-4b9332087f7f" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359121 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535646 true] [17592186359121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535646 true] [17592186359121 1111 #uuid "e93ac296-e56c-5b3a-8af2-25f6b0d6d9ed" 13194139535646 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-138910" 17592186359098 "q-138910/selection" 17592186359099 "q-138910/selection/1417faeeecb9abcec842b42bc312e82d299a8ac8f43684f3e20cdc301cbc99c4" 17592186359100 "q-138910/selection/1ed310c568129094bb708a92e64aac05e57d915c64a6116702028f8effd7d987" 17592186359101 "q-138910/selection/20b7bbf47e4159d425683ef96d3217c51e3972c1a9f75a41eff076cb46db14cf" 175921863591